WEBVTT

00:00.192 --> 00:02.711
- [Voiceover] The number
of branches of an oak tree

00:02.711 --> 00:06.087
and a birch tree since
1950 are represented

00:06.087 --> 00:08.351
by the following tables.

00:08.351 --> 00:11.077
So for the oak tree we
see when time equals zero

00:11.077 --> 00:12.443
has 34 branches.

00:12.443 --> 00:15.131
After three years it has 46 branches,

00:15.131 --> 00:16.605
so on and so forth.

00:16.605 --> 00:18.602
And in the birch tree
they give us similar data.

00:18.602 --> 00:21.609
At the beginning has eight branches.

00:21.609 --> 00:25.521
In 10 years has 33 branches
and they give us all of that.

00:25.521 --> 00:26.751
What I want to think about in this video

00:26.751 --> 00:28.690
is how should we model these?

00:28.690 --> 00:30.295
If we want to model these with functions

00:30.295 --> 00:31.929
and the choices we'll give ourselves,

00:31.929 --> 00:33.241
there are other options,

00:33.241 --> 00:35.211
but the choices we'll give
ourselves in this video

00:35.211 --> 00:38.722
are linear, and linear

00:38.722 --> 00:41.123
versus exponential functions.

00:41.123 --> 00:43.669
Which of these are going to be better

00:45.359 --> 00:48.990
for modeling this data?

00:48.990 --> 00:50.988
So let's first look at the oak tree.

00:50.988 --> 00:53.287
A key to realization is,
whenever I have a fixed

00:53.287 --> 00:54.947
increase in time, so each of these steps,

00:54.947 --> 00:57.466
this is plus three years.

00:57.466 --> 00:59.265
So it's a fixed increase in time.

00:59.265 --> 01:01.135
What happens to my number of branches?

01:01.135 --> 01:03.455
Is it going to be a fixed change,

01:03.455 --> 01:05.597
or roughly a fixed change in which case

01:05.597 --> 01:07.250
a linear model might be good,

01:07.250 --> 01:09.409
or is it going to be a
change that's dependent

01:09.409 --> 01:10.988
on where we were?

01:10.988 --> 01:12.219
So what am I talking about?

01:12.219 --> 01:16.491
So 34 to 46, that is plus 12.

01:16.491 --> 01:20.997
46 to 59 is plus 13.

01:20.997 --> 01:25.290
59 to 70 is plus 11.

01:25.290 --> 01:29.353
70 to 82 is plus 12.

01:29.353 --> 01:31.689
So at first you mention,
well this isn't exactly

01:31.689 --> 01:32.569
a fixed change.

01:32.569 --> 01:37.066
These numbers they seem to
average right around 12,

01:37.066 --> 01:38.687
but when you're looking at real world data

01:38.687 --> 01:40.974
you're never gonna get
something that is exact.

01:40.974 --> 01:43.900
The models are just going
to give us a good fit.

01:43.900 --> 01:45.839
Are going to give us a good approximation

01:45.839 --> 01:49.376
of the behavior of the
number of branches over time.

01:49.376 --> 01:53.515
For me, this is pretty close
to a constant 12 branches

01:53.515 --> 01:54.591
a year.

01:54.591 --> 01:56.889
So I would construct a linear model here.

01:56.889 --> 01:59.871
I would say here branches

01:59.871 --> 02:01.310
as a function of time.

02:01.310 --> 02:04.389
Let me be clear, this
isn't 12 branches per year,

02:04.389 --> 02:07.361
this is 12 branches every three years.

02:07.361 --> 02:09.437
This is 13 branches over three years.

02:09.437 --> 02:11.099
This was 11 branches over three years.

02:11.099 --> 02:14.173
We're going to average 12
branches over three years.

02:14.173 --> 02:16.240
So the number of branches we have,

02:16.240 --> 02:20.036
we're gonna start at 34 branches

02:20.036 --> 02:21.754
and then minus.

02:21.754 --> 02:23.544
If we have 12 branches every three years,

02:23.544 --> 02:26.351
that's four branches every,

02:26.351 --> 02:27.500
or I should say plus.

02:27.500 --> 02:30.495
Four branches every year.

02:30.495 --> 02:31.633
And you could test this out.

02:31.633 --> 02:34.454
B of zero is gonna give us 34 branches.

02:34.454 --> 02:35.735
B of 12.

02:35.735 --> 02:38.935
Let's just really test out
the extreme part of the model.

02:38.935 --> 02:43.888
B of 12 is going to be 34 plus 48,

02:45.768 --> 02:47.872
which is equal to 82.

02:47.872 --> 02:50.217
So this model works quite well.

02:50.217 --> 02:51.076
It's gonna have a couple of places

02:51.076 --> 02:53.258
where it's not exactly fitting the data,

02:53.258 --> 02:55.313
but it fits it quite, quite well.

02:55.313 --> 02:57.019
This is the linear model.

02:57.019 --> 02:58.633
So this one is linear.

02:58.633 --> 03:00.572
We also got the birch tree.

03:00.572 --> 03:04.986
So time equals zero, so
fixed change in time.

03:06.556 --> 03:09.167
Alright, so we have a fixed change in time

03:09.167 --> 03:13.527
every time we are moving
into the future, a decade.

03:13.527 --> 03:15.501
Let's see, our change in branches.

03:15.501 --> 03:17.602
We go from eight to 33.

03:17.602 --> 03:18.577
So what is that?

03:18.577 --> 03:21.758
That is plus 25 branches.

03:21.758 --> 03:24.939
Then we go from 33 to 128.

03:24.939 --> 03:27.170
Well that's way more than 25 branches.

03:27.170 --> 03:29.445
That's going to be what?

03:30.965 --> 03:32.108
Five less than 100.

03:32.108 --> 03:35.858
So that's gonna be plus 95 branches.

03:35.858 --> 03:39.404
So this clearly is not a linear model.

03:39.404 --> 03:41.644
And so let's think in terms
of an exponential model.

03:41.644 --> 03:43.897
How much do we have to
multiply to go from...

03:43.897 --> 03:44.581
Did I do that right?

03:44.581 --> 03:47.101
128 minus, yeah, if it was 133

03:47.101 --> 03:48.123
then it would be 100

03:48.123 --> 03:49.794
and it's five less than that, okay.

03:49.794 --> 03:50.781
So now let's think about it in terms

03:50.781 --> 03:51.767
of an exponential model.

03:51.767 --> 03:53.996
In terms of an exponential model

03:53.996 --> 03:56.055
what do we have to multiply

03:57.285 --> 03:58.559
for each step?

03:58.559 --> 04:00.021
So if we have a constant step in time,

04:00.021 --> 04:02.622
what do we multiply for how much

04:02.622 --> 04:04.282
we increase our branches?

04:04.282 --> 04:06.906
So if we go to eight to 33,

04:08.576 --> 04:11.015
it's gonna be approximately four.

04:11.015 --> 04:13.058
It's gonna be a little bit more than four.

04:13.058 --> 04:16.193
33 to 128,

04:16.193 --> 04:18.921
well that's gonna be a
little bit less than four,

04:18.921 --> 04:20.999
but it's approximately four.

04:20.999 --> 04:24.876
33 times four would be
132, so we're close.

04:24.876 --> 04:29.629
128 to 512, that's exactly four, right?

04:29.629 --> 04:34.187
That's exactly 120 times four is 480

04:34.187 --> 04:37.642
plus 32, yup, that is exactly four.

04:37.642 --> 04:39.051
So times four.

04:39.051 --> 04:41.814
And so it looks like we
keep multiplying by four

04:41.814 --> 04:44.310
every 10 years that go by.

04:44.310 --> 04:47.897
So one way to think about it is

04:47.897 --> 04:51.772
we could say here, BFT, the branches of T,

04:51.772 --> 04:54.950
our initial condition,
our initial state is eight

04:54.950 --> 04:59.069
and now we could say our
common factor is four.

05:01.139 --> 05:04.098
But if we want T to be in years,

05:04.098 --> 05:08.219
well every 10 years we
multiply by a factor of four.

05:08.219 --> 05:11.122
So T has to go to 10 before we increase

05:11.122 --> 05:13.285
the exponent to one,

05:13.285 --> 05:16.220
or has to go to 20 until
this exponent becomes two.

05:16.220 --> 05:19.422
So eight times four to the T over 10 power

05:19.422 --> 05:23.149
seems like a pretty good model.

05:23.149 --> 05:25.703
And you could even verify
this for yourself if you like.

05:25.703 --> 05:28.872
Try out what B of 30 is going to be.

05:28.872 --> 05:33.409
B of 30 would be eight times four.

05:33.409 --> 05:36.571
30 divided by 10 to the third power

05:36.571 --> 05:38.333
and what is that going to be?

05:38.333 --> 05:42.396
That's going to be four
to the third is 64.

05:42.396 --> 05:43.815
64.

05:43.815 --> 05:46.623
Eight times 64 is,

05:46.623 --> 05:48.375
it's 480 plus 32.

05:48.375 --> 05:49.839
It is 512.

05:49.839 --> 05:53.189
So once again, this exponential model

05:53.189 --> 05:55.892
for this data does a pretty good job.
